        }
      },
      "snippet": {
        "text": "Preeclampsia (PE) and fetal growth restriction (FGR) are considered among perinatal high-risk conditions that despite having a multifactorial and only partially understood origin, share an anatomical feature -an abnormal placentation [1][2][3] . PE is part of the hypertensive disorders of pregnancy and is characterized by new-onset hypertension and proteinuria, occurring usually after 20 weeks' gestation. It affects 4-7% of the pregnant population. FGR, on the other hand, refers to a condition in which a fetus is unable to achieve its genetically determined potential size. This is defined as growth below the 10th percentile for weight of all fetuses at that gestational age. There are predictive models for these conditions but their predictive values are discrete, implementing them is expensive and currently there is no successful therapeutical intervention for preventing or delaying their onset or their poor outcome [3][4][5][6][7][8][9] .",
