      "paper": {
        "corpusId": "19306329",
        "title": "Homocysteine in pregnancies complicated by preeclampsia with and without IUGR: a comparison with normotensive pregnant women with isolated IUGR and healthy pregnant women",
        "authors": [
          "M. Laskowska",
          "J. Oleszczuk"
        ],
        "openAccessInfo": {
          "license": "CCBY",
          "status": "GOLD",
          "disclaimer": "Notice: This snippet is extracted from the open access paper or abstract available at https://api.unpaywall.org/v2/10.4236/OJOG.2011.14037?email=<INSERT_YOUR_EMAIL> or https://doi.org/10.4236/OJOG.2011.14037, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."
        }
      },
      "snippet": {
        "text": "The study was carried out on 49 pregnant women with normotensive pregnancies complicated by intrauterine growth restricted fetuses (IUGR group), and 66 patients with pregnancy complicated by severe preeclampsia. In the group of preeclamptic women were 31 patients with preeclampsia complicated by intrauterine fetal growth restriction (PRE-IUGR group) and 35 preeclamptic patients with appropriate-for-gestational-age fetuses (PRE group). Among preeclamptic women there were 6 patients with HELLP syndrome (all in the PRE-IUGR group) and 4 patients with eclampsia (3 in the PRE-IUGR group and 1 in the PRE group). One patient from the IUGR group had fetal intrauterine death in the week 38 th of gestation. The weight of her stillborn infant was 2060 g. \n\nThe control group consisted of 47 healthy normotensive pregnant patients with singleton uncomplicated pregnancies without any renal, cardiac and vascular diseases and with normal laboratory tests and with appropriate-for-gestational-age weight infants (CON group). \n\nFetal biometry was based on non-invasive ultrasound method and comprised the estimation of gestational age in early gestation and diagnosis of fetal intrauterine growth restriction by monitoring fetal growth later in the second or third trimester of pregnancy. \n\nIntrauterine growth restricted fetuses (IUGR) were classified as such according to ultrasonographic measurement when the weight of the fetus was lower than expected in relation to the gestational age, as determined by the standard curves characteristic of the Polish population, when the fetus was below the 5 th centile for gestational age and diagnosis was confirmed by the infant's weight at birth according to the Bretelle et al. [5]. \n\nThe exclusion criteria for women whose pregnancies were complicated by IUGR fetuses were the presence of a congenital malformation or chromosomal abnormality in the fetus, recent cytomegalovirus infection or drug or alcohol abuse during pregnancy. Pregnant women with multiple pregnancies were also excluded from this study.",
