  "url": "https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371/journal.pone.0284230",
  "title": "The association between maternal intra-abdominal pressure and hypertension in pregnancy",
  "content": "Citation and publication\n- Jayasundara S, Goonewardene M, Dassanayake L. The association between maternal intra-abdominal pressure and hypertension in pregnancy. PLoS ONE. Published Oct 18, 2023. DOI: 10.1371/journal.pone.0284230. Received Mar 26, 2023; Accepted Oct 3, 2023. Open access; no specific funding; authors declare no competing interests.\n\nObjective\n- Determine antepartum and postpartum intra-abdominal pressure (IAP) in women undergoing non-emergency cesarean delivery (CD) and assess association between IAP and hypertension in pregnancy (HIP).\n\nStudy design and participants\n- Cross-sectional study (enrolment 2016\u20132017) at Teaching Hospital, Mahamodara, Galle, Sri Lanka.\n- n = 70 singleton pregnancies undergoing antepartum, non-emergency CD: 55 normotensive (78.6%); 15 HIP (21.4%) \u2014 HIP breakdown: gestational hypertension n=5; preeclampsia n=8; eclampsia n=1; chronic hypertension n=1.\n- Exclusions: Category 1 CD, CDs during active labor, vaginal deliveries.\n\nIAP measurement protocol\n- Intravesical pressure used as surrogate for IAP following World Abdominal Compartment Society recommendations.\n- Procedure: 16-Fr Foley catheter, bladder emptied, 25 mL saline inflation, supine position, mid-axillary zero reference; three consecutive end-expiration readings; mean recorded.\n- Timing: before spinal/general anesthesia (pre-op) and repeated 6\u20138 hours postoperatively when pain VAS < 3.\n\nStatistical methods\n- SPSS v22.0. Normality by Kolmogorov\u2013Smirnov. Group comparisons via independent t-test and Chi-square. Two-tailed p<0.05 considered significant.\n- Multivariable least-squares linear regression for three continuous dependent variables: antepartum IAP, postpartum IAP, and IAP difference (antepartum \u2212 postpartum).\n- Candidate covariates: hypertension (binary), maternal BMI, birthweight, parity. Gestational age removed due to high collinearity with birthweight (Pearson r = 0.78).\n- Model selection used adjusted R2; squared transformations tested.\n\nKey results \u2014 descriptive\n- Normotensive group (mean gestational age 38.2 weeks; 95% CI 37.9\u201338.6):\n  - Mean antepartum IAP = 12.7 mmHg (95% CI 11.6\u201313.8).\n  - Mean postpartum IAP = 7.3 mmHg (95% CI 6.5\u20138.0).\n- HIP group (mean gestational age 34.3 weeks; mean birthweight 1,886.66 g):\n  - Mean antepartum IAP = 12.5 mmHg (95% CI 10.0\u201315.0).\n  - Mean postpartum IAP = 9.2 mmHg (95% CI 7.3\u201311.1).\n\nKey results \u2014 multivariable regression (models adjusted as above)\n- Antepartum IAP model:\n  - Best model included hypertension and birthweight. Adjusted R2 = 0.032.\n  - Birthweight significant predictor (p = 0.042).\n  - Hypertension coefficient = +1.617 mmHg (p = 0.268) \u2014 positive association but not statistically significant.\n- IAP difference (antepartum \u2212 postpartum) model:\n  - Best model included hypertension and birthweight^2. R2 = 0.190.\n  - Hypertension coefficient = \u22121.013 mmHg (p = 0.179) \u2014 reduction in IAP from antepartum to postpartum was smaller in HIP but not statistically significant.\n- Postpartum IAP model:\n  - Best model included hypertension and birthweight. R2 = 0.059.\n  - Hypertension coefficient = +2.519 mmHg; statistically significant (p = 0.018).\n\nMain conclusions\n- In term normotensive pregnancies, antepartum mean IAP (~12.7 mmHg) falls within the intra-abdominal hypertension (IAH) range reported for non-pregnant populations; postpartum mean IAP ~7.3 mmHg.\n- HIP is associated with higher antepartum and postpartum IAP and with a smaller reduction in IAP after delivery compared with normotensive pregnancies.\n- After adjustment, postpartum IAP was significantly higher in HIP (coef +2.519 mmHg, p = 0.018); antepartum IAP and IAP reduction showed positive/negative associations with HIP respectively but did not reach statistical significance (p = 0.268 and p = 0.179).\n- Authors note limited HIP sample size and recommend prospective studies with serial IAP measurements across gestation and postpartum.\n\nOther notable details\n- IAP ranges from other studies: antepartum in singleton normotensive pregnancies previously reported 7.3\u201314.1 mmHg; postpartum 3.7\u201310.8 mmHg.\n- Clinical implications discussed: hypothesis that elevated IAP may contribute to preeclampsia pathophysiology; historical and small pilot data on abdominal decompression and prone positioning suggest potential for IAP-lowering interventions, but evidence is limited.",
  "description": "Introduction Pregnancy leads to a state of chronically increased intra\u2010abdominal pressure (IAP) caused by a growing fetus, fluid, and tissue. Increased intra-abdominal pressure is leading to state of Intra-Abdominal Hypertension (IAH) and Abdominal Compartment Syndrome. Clinical features and risk factors of preeclampsia is comparable to abdominal compartment syndrome. IAP may be associated with the hypertension in pregnancy (HIP).   Objectives The study aimed to determine the antepartum and postpartum IAP levels in women undergoing caesarean delivery (CD) and association between hypertension in pregnancy, and antepartum and postpartum IAP levels in women undergoing CD.   Method Seventy pregnant women (55 normotensive, 15 HIP) undergoing antepartum, non-emergency CD, had their intravesical pressure measured before and after the CD, the intravesical pressure measurements obtained with the patient in the supine position were considered to correspond to the IAP. Multivariable linear regression models were used to study associations between intraabdominal pressure and baseline characteristics in normotensive pregnancies and hypertensive pregnancies.   Results In normotensive pregnancies at mean gestation age of 38.2 weeks (95%CI 37.9 to 38.6), mean antepartum IAP was 12.7 mmHg(95%CI 11.6 to 13.8) and the mean postpartum IAP was 7.3 mmHg (95% CI 11.6 to 13.8). Multivariable linear regression models showed HIP group antepartum IAP positively associated with coefficient value of 1.617 (p = 0.268) comparing with normotensive pregnancy group. Postpartum IAP in HIP group positively associated with coefficient value of 2.519 (p = 0.018) comparing with normotensive pregnancy group. IAP difference is negatively associated with HIP (coefficient -1.013, p = 0.179).   Conclusion In normotensive pregnancies at term, the IAP was in the IAH range of the non-pregnant population. Higher Antepartum IAP and Postpartum IAP are associated with HIP. Reduction of IAP from antepartum period to postpartum period was less with HIP.",
