  "title": "Abdominal decompression in normal pregnancy",
  "content": "Topic: Prophylactic abdominal decompression in normal pregnancy \u2014 Cochrane review (search updated 2 Feb 2012)\n\nScope and objective\n- Assess effects of prophylactic antenatal abdominal decompression on admission for pre\u2010eclampsia, fetal growth, perinatal morbidity and mortality, and childhood development in healthy pregnant women.\n- Search: Cochrane Pregnancy and Childbirth Group Trials Register (2 Feb 2012). No language restrictions. No new trials identified in the update.\n\nIntervention (definition)\n- Abdominal decompression: rigid dome around the abdomen with airtight suit; intermittent negative pressure (typically \u221250 to \u2212100 mmHg) applied for 15\u201330 seconds each minute for 30 minutes, once to thrice daily, or applied with contractions during labour. Intended to improve uteroplacental blood flow.\n\nIncluded trials (3)\n1. Coxon 1973 (UK) \u2014 randomized allocation; primigravidae single fetus; decompression from ~28 weeks, 15 s/min for 30 min twice weekly. Comparison: \u221270 mmHg (n=200) versus \u221220 mmHg 'control' (n=211). Outcomes: BP, admission for pre\u2010eclampsia, birthweight, placental weight, perinatal mortality. Note: control (\u221220 mmHg) may not be an inert placebo.\n2. Liddicoat 1968 (South Africa) \u2014 randomized allocation; antenatal decompression versus routine antenatal physiotherapy classes. Offspring evaluated blind at 1, 4, 9 months and 3 years (Merrill-Palmer at 3 years). High dropout: 45% at 9 months, 56% by 3 years.\n3. Hofmeyr 1990 \u2014 review of hospital notes from the Liddicoat cohort to report perinatal data (early 1960s cohort). ~23% of results unobtainable.\n\nExcluded\n- Mathews 1968: excluded because no clinically relevant outcomes reported (20 women in labour; fetal scalp measures only).\n\nRisk of bias\n- Randomization/allocation concealment judged adequa...
